-`windows_minimize` accepts `popin` or `fade`. `popin` scales the window to `scale` while it fades; `fade` leaves its geometry alone. `anchor` picks the point a `popin` collapse centres on: `"center"` keeps the window's own centre, `"top_left"` keeps its top-left corner still, and `"bottom_center"` moves it toward the bottom centre of the output's usable area. The restore plays the same timeline in reverse, so a window grows back from exactly where it left. Animation time is spent on the live window: `minimized`, `visible`, focus handoff, and taskbars change at once, while the window is still leaving. Custom effects do not apply here, because a transition animates one of the fixed event slots and minimize has none of its own.
+`windows_minimize` accepts `popin` or `fade`. `popin` scales the window to `scale` while it fades; `fade` leaves its geometry alone. `anchor` picks where a `popin` collapse goes: `"center"` keeps the window's own centre, `"top_left"` keeps its top-left corner still, and `"bottom_center"` rests the scaled window's bottom edge on the bottom of the output's usable area. The restore plays the same timeline in reverse, so a window grows back from exactly where it left. Animation time is spent on the live window: `minimized`, `visible`, focus handoff, and taskbars change at once, while the window is still leaving. A workspace switch or an overview reopening mid-fade can therefore still draw it for the rest of the timeline. A fading window takes no pointer input. Custom effects do not apply here, because a transition animates one of the fixed event slots and minimize has none of its own.
 
 `windows_in` accepts `popin`, `zoom`, `slide`, `fade`, or `none`. `windows_out` accepts `fade`, `slide`, `popin`, or `zoom`. `workspaces` accepts `slide` or `reveal` (see [Workspace reveal](#workspace-reveal)). `scale` applies to `popin`: an opening window grows from it to full size, and a closing window shrinks toward it, while both fade.
 
